<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Callejón de Huaylas</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Callejón de Huaylas is the valley of the upper Santa River in the Ancash region of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, running between the snow-capped Cordillera Blanca and the Cordillera Negra. It was a heartland of the Recuay culture in the first centuries CE and lies close to Chavín de Huántar. Its main town is Huaraz, and in 1970 an earthquake-triggered avalanche buried the town of Yungay.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/callejon-de-huaylas.jpg" alt="Callejón de Huaylas" width="320" height="239" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TMbux · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caminho_Laguna_Par%C3%B3n_(Cordillera_Blanca)_-_panoramio_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The valley of the Santa River, in Quechua Sancta, owes its name, the Alley of Huaylas, to its place between two mountain ranges and to a territorial division of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru</a>. It stretches for 150 km from Lake Conococha, the river's main source, falling from 4,000 m above sea level to 2,000 m past Caraz. The Cordillera Blanca to the east holds Nevado Huascarán (6,746 m), the highest peak of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, while the Cordillera Negra to the west rises over 4,000 m but typically gets no snow.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In Guitarrero cave, on the eastern slopes of the Cordillera Negra, Thomas F. Lynch and his team found preserved plants, among them domesticated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> and coastal species, whose radiocarbon dates show human habitation at least 10,000 years old, and Quebrada Perrón served for thousands of years as a stone workshop. The ceremonial complex of Chavín de Huantar, on a pass between the Conchucos Valley and the Callejón, developed from 900 BCE and spread its influence as far as the Jequetepeque and Lambayeque valleys.</p><p>As Chavín faded from about 200 BCE, settlements moved to hilltops and defensible sites, and the Recuay culture, known for kaolin pottery painted in red, white and black and for underground tombs, came to dominate the Santa Valley. From around 600 CE the Wari became preeminent, as surface chullpa tombs with Wari-style pottery at Marca Jirca and temples and a road at Honco Pampa show, though the region seems largely to have governed itself. After Wari influence faded by the eleventh century, the valley's importance faded from the archaeological record.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-san-martin">José de San Martín</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a> used the valley as a base during the Wars of Independence, and in 1885 Pedro Pablo Atusparía led a peasant uprising there. On 31 May 1970 a landslide caused by the Ancash earthquake buried Yungay, leaving only 92 survivors, and partly destroyed Huaraz, Caraz and Aija.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Huaraz, the capital of the Ancash Region, is the largest city, at 3,000 m. Most people are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> and Spanish-speaking small farmers in mountain villages, with Spanish-speaking mestizos in the towns along the river. Huascarán National Park, in the valley, was designated a World Biosphere Reserve by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> in 1977.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Callejón de Huaylas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Recuay culture arose around the start of the Common Era in the <b>Callejón de Huaylas</b> and the upper valleys of Ancash, in the same region where Chavín had flourished.</blockquote><a href="/state/recuay">Recuay</a></li></ul></section>
